  "honest_disclosure": {
    "synthetic_attenuation": "behavior=mean over D=24 uniform vars concentrates near 0.5 (CLT, std~0.059); in 4000 samples the max behavior reached is ~0.70, so the global peak at b=0.90 is NEVER sampled (0 samples in [0.85,0.92]). The metric therefore measures only the PARTIAL dip within the reachable band b in [0.30,0.70], a heavily attenuated shadow of the knob d. This is why metric_at_dstar=0.0153 is ~8x smaller than the naive expectation 0.8*d~0.13. RANK transfers (Spearman=1.0, strictly monotone) but CALIBRATED MAGNITUDE does NOT.",
    "reproduces_threshold_caveat": "reproduces_threshold=True is near-tautological for a strictly-monotone metric: metric_at_dstar is BY CONSTRUCTION the d=0.16 value, so (metric>=metric_at_dstar) <=> (d>=0.16) holds automatically given monotonicity. It confirms the metric is consistent with the known threshold ORDERING, not that it independently rediscovers d*=0.16 as a special inflection.",
    "noise": "At d>=0.20 the 5-seed 95%CI includes 0 (finite-sample envelope max is noisy and upward-biased at sparse high-behavior bins). Point estimates above d* are not significantly separated from each other.",
    "real_task_axis_mismatch": "Real-task behavior descriptor is 2D (eff_mem_norm, std(leak)) PCA-projected to 1D - a DIFFERENT axis from synthetic behavior=mean. Raw real dip values are NOT on the same calibrated scale as metric_at_dstar; placing them on the synthetic d-axis would be unjustified. Report them as same-method comparison ONLY (E-A vs flip_flop), not as numeric d placement."
